  "enhanced_information": "Chronic alcohol abuse can lead to thiamine deficiency, resulting in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome with characteristic findings like atrophic mammillary bodies with brown-tan discoloration on autopsy. This syndrome can cause abnormal CSF IgG protein levels in vivo. Liver dysfunction from chronic alcohol abuse can decrease aldolase B activity, potentially leading to abnormal results related to nervous system findings on autopsy. Therefore, CSF IgG protein and Aldolase B activity are the most likely tests to produce abnormal results in vivo with respect to the nervous system findings on autopsy."
